00:24:38.590 --> 00:24:41.350
The budget will be read into introduction tonight.

00:24:41.750 --> 00:24:46.380
We will have a work session on September 18th at 11 a.m.

00:24:46.950 --> 00:24:51.220
where the budget and, uh, uh, other, uh, attached.

00:24:51.940 --> 00:24:56.850
Items will be presented to the council and council members will have an

00:24:56.850 --> 00:25:01.810
opportunity to ask questions, uh, also share any amendments that they plan

00:25:01.810 --> 00:25:05.720
to make it at the, the final session, which will be September 24th when

00:25:05.720 --> 00:25:09.460
we will have the regular council meeting when we will approve the budget.

00:25:09.820 --> 00:25:13.480
So, for everyone's attention, council members and public,

00:25:13.820 --> 00:25:15.950
be aware that that's how we're going to be handling things.

00:25:16.280 --> 00:25:17.970
Now, we will move on to presentation.

00:25:18.060 --> 00:25:20.680
7A is a presentation on the sound diplomacy,

00:25:20.700 --> 00:25:21.040
music.

00:25:21.240 --> 00:25:24.850
Impact study as I stated earlier, among other things,

00:25:25.030 --> 00:25:29.450
September is Music Month, so we felt that this would be an appropriate time

00:25:29.470 --> 00:25:33.980
to have a presentation on the music audit with us tonight is Matt Mandrella,

00:25:34.030 --> 00:25:34.340
and Mr.

00:25:34.580 --> 00:25:36.020
Mandrella, time is yours.

00:25:36.430 --> 00:25:39.500
Alrighty, um, good evening, and, um, it's,

00:25:39.590 --> 00:25:42.690
uh, it's a very exciting time for music in Huntsville and really appreciate

00:25:42.710 --> 00:25:46.480
the opportunity to, uh, share a quick update on where we are and,

00:25:46.510 --> 00:25:50.060
uh, what the latest data tells us about our music sector's growth and its

00:25:50.060 --> 00:25:50.420
future.

00:25:51.080 --> 00:25:55.350
Back in 2018, launching Huntsville's first music audit was a bit of a leap

00:25:55.350 --> 00:25:56.060
of faith.

00:25:56.440 --> 00:26:00.840
Uh, we were the first city in the United States to take this kind of comprehensive

00:26:00.840 --> 00:26:04.830
data-driven approach to intentionally growing our music ecosystem.

00:26:05.120 --> 00:26:08.950
We wanted to better understand Huntsville's own creative identity and how

00:26:08.950 --> 00:26:10.750
music could strengthen our quality of life,

00:26:10.880 --> 00:26:14.190
diversify our economy, attract talent, and help shape the,

00:26:14.240 --> 00:26:15.430
uh, city we were becoming.

00:26:16.040 --> 00:26:18.660
The first audit identified strengths that were already here,

00:26:19.010 --> 00:26:22.400
but it also revealed opportunities that we had not fully tapped into.

00:26:22.800 --> 00:26:26.190
It gave us a roadmap of actionable recommendations that the city and our

00:26:26.190 --> 00:26:28.590
community partners have steadily worked on advancing.

00:26:29.070 --> 00:26:31.820
And that worked helped lead to the creation of uh my role,

00:26:32.190 --> 00:26:36.060
uh, the Huntsville Music Office, major investments like the Orion Amphitheater,

00:26:36.150 --> 00:26:39.540
and a much more intentional approach to growing our ecosystem.

00:26:39.590 --> 00:26:43.940
And so 8 years later we went back to sound diplomacy to ask how far we'd

00:26:43.940 --> 00:26:47.300
come, and the, uh, data tells a pretty remarkable story.

00:26:49.350 --> 00:26:50.100
Let's see.

00:26:53.130 --> 00:26:54.550
Uh, let's start with the big picture.

00:26:54.930 --> 00:27:01.150
In 2018, Huntsville's music ecosystem generated approximately 191.2 million

00:27:01.150 --> 00:27:04.130
in total economic output, and that's adjusted to inflation.

00:27:04.540 --> 00:27:08.340
Uh, today that number has grown to 278.2 million.

00:27:08.610 --> 00:27:11.120
Uh, that's an increase of roughly 45%.

00:27:11.610 --> 00:27:13.680
And when we talk about the music economy,

00:27:14.060 --> 00:27:15.960
We're talking about much more than concerts.

00:27:16.010 --> 00:27:19.910
It includes artists, venues, festivals, music education,

00:27:20.010 --> 00:27:24.750
retail, radio studios, and all the businesses that support music activity.

00:27:25.090 --> 00:27:29.320
Uh, the simplest takeaway from this is that music is now definitely a significant

00:27:29.320 --> 00:27:31.850
and measurable part of Huntsville's overall economy.

00:27:35.250 --> 00:27:36.640
And talk about jobs.

00:27:36.770 --> 00:27:40.120
I think one of the most exciting indicators about this is the employment

00:27:40.120 --> 00:27:40.570
growth.

00:27:41.010 --> 00:27:45.590
The original audit identified 986 direct music-related jobs.

00:27:45.970 --> 00:27:51.290
Today, Huntsville's music ecosystem supports 3,047 jobs across the broader

00:27:51.290 --> 00:27:56.960
regional economy, and that's 2,424 jobs directly within the music sector.

00:27:57.360 --> 00:28:01.650
That direct workforce is nearly 2.5 times the size it was when our work

00:28:01.650 --> 00:28:06.110
began, and so that's an increase of about 145% in the employment sector.

00:28:06.440 --> 00:28:10.580
And so that means real employment opportunities not only for artists and

00:28:10.580 --> 00:28:13.400
performers, but also venue and production professionals,

00:28:13.520 --> 00:28:16.870
educators, technicians, promoters, and many others.

00:28:17.200 --> 00:28:20.190
Uh, so the ecosystem hasn't simply just gotten busier.

00:28:20.480 --> 00:28:24.110
Uh, we've grown it larger, deeper, and increasingly professionalized.

00:28:28.070 --> 00:28:31.210
Another way that economists measure the strength of an industry is through

00:28:31.230 --> 00:28:33.310
gross value added or GVA.

00:28:33.590 --> 00:28:37.030
So essentially that's the value the sector itself contributes to the economy.

00:28:37.630 --> 00:28:42.120
Today Huntsville's music ecosystem contributes approximately $162 million

00:28:42.290 --> 00:28:46.380
in gross value added, and this chart shows us where that value is being

00:28:46.380 --> 00:28:47.020
generated.

00:28:47.470 --> 00:28:50.770
Uh, creation is the artists and the songwriters making the music.

00:28:51.070 --> 00:28:55.070
Production is the studios, producers, and services that turn that creativity

00:28:55.070 --> 00:28:56.470
into a finished product.

00:28:56.830 --> 00:28:59.810
Distribution is how music reaches audiences and markets.

00:29:00.070 --> 00:29:02.080
Exhibition is the live side of the industry,

00:29:02.190 --> 00:29:03.540
such as festivals, venues.

00:29:03.680 --> 00:29:07.890
Concerts and promoters and Transversal is the support system around it all,

00:29:07.910 --> 00:29:10.660
such as music education, management, equipment,

00:29:10.830 --> 00:29:12.980
nonprofits, and other professional services.

00:29:13.310 --> 00:29:16.460
Uh, as you can see, exhibition and support services account for the vast

00:29:16.460 --> 00:29:19.900
majority of, uh, the economic value currently being generated.

00:29:20.260 --> 00:29:22.750
And so that also reflects something we already know.

00:29:23.020 --> 00:29:26.880
Huntsville has become very good at presenting and supporting live music,

00:29:27.180 --> 00:29:30.570
uh, but it also highlights one of our clearest opportunities for future

00:29:30.570 --> 00:29:33.730
growth, and that's the, uh, creation and production aspects of things.

00:29:33.780 --> 00:29:37.370
And so the next phase of our work is not just gonna be about bringing more

00:29:37.370 --> 00:29:40.970
music to Huntsville, it's gonna be helping more music get created here,

00:29:41.230 --> 00:29:44.970
recorded here, developed here, and ultimately commercialized from here.

00:29:49.380 --> 00:29:52.570
Uh, we can also physically see the growth of the ecosystem across the city

00:29:52.570 --> 00:29:53.710
since the initial audit.

00:29:53.980 --> 00:29:58.370
In 2018, we had 163 music-related assets mapped throughout the,

00:29:58.460 --> 00:29:59.050
uh, city.

00:29:59.370 --> 00:30:01.770
Uh, today we've grown that number to 215,

00:30:01.900 --> 00:30:03.840
so that's a 32% increase.

00:30:04.140 --> 00:30:07.650
Uh, those assets include venues, recording and rehearsal spaces,

00:30:07.820 --> 00:30:10.680
music education spots, retail production companies,

00:30:10.900 --> 00:30:13.130
and other businesses and organizations that make the,

00:30:13.220 --> 00:30:14.850
uh, music ecosystem function.

00:30:15.270 --> 00:30:18.850
So this isn't simply just a story about um having more concerts.

00:30:19.110 --> 00:30:22.100
Uh, it's infrastructure underneath Huntsville's music industry that has

00:30:22.100 --> 00:30:23.540
grown significantly too.

00:30:26.160 --> 00:30:27.820
Uh, here's one I'm especially proud of.

00:30:28.090 --> 00:30:32.300
Huntsville now hosts more than 2200 music events every year.

00:30:32.640 --> 00:30:36.080
Uh, that's everything from major nights at the Orion and the Von Braun Center

00:30:36.280 --> 00:30:38.910
to local venues, festivals, neighborhood events,

00:30:39.200 --> 00:30:41.820
songwriter nights, and grassroots community performances.

00:30:42.040 --> 00:30:44.730
And that variety is, uh, important and it matters.

00:30:44.960 --> 00:30:47.550
A strong music city isn't really defined by one venue,

00:30:47.680 --> 00:30:49.450
one artist, or one festival.

00:30:49.750 --> 00:30:52.910
Uh, it's defined by the depth and diversity of opportunities happening throughout

00:30:52.910 --> 00:30:54.070
our entire community.

00:30:56.080 --> 00:30:59.150
And as part of the sound diplomacy work they did some specific case studies

00:30:59.150 --> 00:31:01.630
into our partner venues and events.

00:31:01.720 --> 00:31:05.430
The von Braun Center, as we all know, has been the centerpiece of Huntsville's

00:31:05.430 --> 00:31:08.100
music and entertainment landscape for now more than 50 years,

00:31:08.160 --> 00:31:11.460
and its economic impact continues to be extraordinary.

00:31:11.780 --> 00:31:18.030
In 2025 alone, BBC concerts generated approximately $37.5 million in economic

00:31:18.030 --> 00:31:21.710
impact and an estimated $5.3 million in local tax revenue.

00:31:22.180 --> 00:31:25.090
And that activity stands well beyond the walls of the BBC.

00:31:25.140 --> 00:31:26.760
It brings people downtown.

00:31:27.020 --> 00:31:30.820
It builds hotels, restaurants, and supports businesses throughout our community.

00:31:31.220 --> 00:31:34.670
Uh, the BBC remains one of the clearest examples of how music and entertainment

00:31:34.670 --> 00:31:38.210
infrastructure can generate real economic value for Huntsville.

00:31:40.940 --> 00:31:43.850
Uh, the Orion Amphitheater has expanded that impact even further.

00:31:44.210 --> 00:31:49.220
In its 1st 18 months, the Orion welcomed approximately 315,000 attendees

00:31:49.450 --> 00:31:52.920
and generated 85.2 million in economic output.

00:31:53.630 --> 00:31:58.180
nearly 150,000 of those tickets were sold to non-local residents and attendees.

00:31:58.270 --> 00:32:02.110
So that's significant because those people are choosing to come to Huntsville

00:32:02.110 --> 00:32:05.300
because of music and also spending money throughout our community while

00:32:05.300 --> 00:32:06.900
they're here outside of the amphitheater.

00:32:06.950 --> 00:32:10.980
And so that's a very form of a very tangible form of cultural tourism that

00:32:10.980 --> 00:32:11.970
we're able to prove.

00:32:14.060 --> 00:32:17.410
The South Star Music Festival demonstrated Huntsville's potential on another

00:32:17.410 --> 00:32:17.930
scale.

00:32:18.300 --> 00:32:21.420
Uh, the festival, even though we lost the second day due to,

00:32:21.440 --> 00:32:26.270
uh, the hurricane, it generated an estimated $24.1 million in economic impact

00:32:26.460 --> 00:32:30.210
in that single day, uh, while the national attention surrounding its launch

00:32:30.210 --> 00:32:35.170
generated approximately $218 million in earned media value.

00:32:35.500 --> 00:32:39.210
Uh, those are remarkable numbers, and when we put that together with the

00:32:39.210 --> 00:32:41.790
BBC and the Orion, they tell a pretty clear story that,

00:32:41.820 --> 00:32:42.210
um.

00:32:42.640 --> 00:32:45.100
Huntsville isn't simply hosting more shows.

00:32:45.360 --> 00:32:46.760
Uh, music is creating jobs.

00:32:46.810 --> 00:32:51.150
It's driving tourism and generating real economic and cultural value for

00:32:51.150 --> 00:32:51.790
our city.

00:32:54.390 --> 00:32:58.860
Industry partnerships, we, uh, building a sustainable city can't only be

00:32:58.860 --> 00:33:01.210
about bringing great artists and events to Huntsville.

00:33:01.390 --> 00:33:04.830
We also have to create pathways for Huntsville artists and music professionals

00:33:04.830 --> 00:33:05.970
to build careers here.

00:33:06.400 --> 00:33:09.780
Uh, that's why relationships and partnerships we've established with organizations

00:33:09.780 --> 00:33:12.460
like the Recording Academy, Universal Music Group,

00:33:12.510 --> 00:33:14.820
and East Irish Studios in Nashville are so important.

00:33:14.970 --> 00:33:15.430
They, uh.

00:33:15.940 --> 00:33:18.960
Help connect our local creative community to industry expertise,

00:33:19.250 --> 00:33:21.290
professional networks, and opportunities that,

00:33:21.450 --> 00:33:23.770
frankly, they don't have here in Huntsville currently,

00:33:24.050 --> 00:33:27.400
and they help us build and strengthen the business side infrastructure around

00:33:27.400 --> 00:33:31.040
our ecosystem needed for that long-term sustainable growth to,

00:33:31.060 --> 00:33:35.040
uh, to keep this moving upward, uh, because ultimately we want Huntsville

00:33:35.040 --> 00:33:38.090
to be more than just a great place to play and experience music,

00:33:38.290 --> 00:33:41.080
we want it to be a great place to build a career in music.

00:33:42.270 --> 00:33:44.170
And so what is next?

00:33:44.710 --> 00:33:46.660
Uh, that brings us to that very important question.

00:33:46.790 --> 00:33:50.500
The, um, updated audit gives us another roadmap that we'll be focusing on.

00:33:50.590 --> 00:33:53.040
We need to, uh, continue investing in the things that have,

00:33:53.070 --> 00:33:56.140
uh, led to our success to get us here like the artist support,

00:33:56.270 --> 00:33:59.490
music education, the festival, and the venue infrastructure,

00:33:59.750 --> 00:34:00.940
uh, but we also need to really.

00:34:01.010 --> 00:34:03.630
Focus and continue expanding the business side of the industry.

00:34:03.720 --> 00:34:06.790
We need more studios, more managers, and more agents,

00:34:06.840 --> 00:34:10.949
and just more overall licensing support and other professional services

00:34:10.949 --> 00:34:14.870
that are needed to help artists sustain those careers here in Huntsville

00:34:14.870 --> 00:34:17.420
to where they don't feel like they need to move to Nashville.

00:34:17.880 --> 00:34:21.190
Uh, Huntsville's become very, very good at bringing music here,

00:34:21.639 --> 00:34:25.420
and our next opportunity is helping more music careers and businesses grow

00:34:25.560 --> 00:34:26.270
from here.

00:34:26.730 --> 00:34:29.360
Uh, that doesn't mean moving away from live music by any means.

00:34:29.409 --> 00:34:33.219
It just means building on the success we already have so that more of the

00:34:33.219 --> 00:34:35.760
talent, intellectual property, businesses,

00:34:35.810 --> 00:34:39.120
and economic values surrounding music can be created,

00:34:39.330 --> 00:34:41.199
developed, and retained here in Huntsville.

00:34:41.489 --> 00:34:44.239
Uh, we've built the live music economy and now we need to,

00:34:44.330 --> 00:34:46.120
uh, build more of the industry around it.

00:34:49.179 --> 00:34:51.980
Uh, one final piece of our story that I'm especially proud of,

00:34:52.260 --> 00:34:54.820
uh, Huntsville is now helping lead this work nationally.

00:34:55.230 --> 00:34:58.490
Uh, the Huntsville Music Office is a proud founding member of the Association

00:34:58.490 --> 00:34:59.390
of Music Offices.

00:34:59.460 --> 00:35:02.250
That's a, uh, recently launched new national network,

00:35:02.380 --> 00:35:05.720
uh, bringing public music offices and industry partners together from across

00:35:05.740 --> 00:35:09.690
the country to share best practices, strengthen connections between cities

00:35:09.690 --> 00:35:13.730
and states, and, uh, work on advancing the role that music can play in economic

00:35:13.730 --> 00:35:16.010
development, quality of life, and community building.

00:35:16.460 --> 00:35:19.250
Uh, for Huntsville, it's another sign that the work we've started here 8

00:35:19.250 --> 00:35:23.100
years ago is now part of a much broader national conversation,

00:35:23.660 --> 00:35:27.240
and we're at the table alongside music offices and partners from across

00:35:27.260 --> 00:35:30.810
the country and industry partners like the Recording Academy who produces

00:35:30.810 --> 00:35:31.960
the Grammy Awards.

00:35:32.240 --> 00:35:35.750
Uh, that matters because cities and states are increasingly looking at music

00:35:35.750 --> 00:35:39.440
now, not just as entertainment, but as part of their broader economic and

00:35:39.440 --> 00:35:41.020
community development strategy and.

00:35:41.410 --> 00:35:45.090
It's exciting because Huntsville isn't just participating in that conversation,

00:35:45.210 --> 00:35:48.400
